{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1PrLfx8f9mhaJmmij5n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up on this one. I haven't written or compiled a proof, so `/work/putnam_2022_a4.lean` is unchanged.\n\nThe statement is true, and I worked out the math, but a Lean proof would be a large project, and I didn't judge it feasible in this session.\n\n**The math (checked by hand):**\n\n1. **Unpacking `k`.** Let L be the least l ≥ 0 with X_l ≤ X_{l+1}. The valid m are exactly m ≤ L, so `sSup` gives k ω = `Set.Iic L`, or `univ` if there is no such L. This is the Putnam problem with indices shifted to start at 0. The `>` versus `<` difference only matters on a probability-zero set.\n\n2. **Reduce to a sum.** Since S = Σ_i X_i/2^{i+1} · 1{i ≤ L}, and {i ≤ L} = {X_0 > X_1 > … > X_i}, linearity and monotone convergence give E[S] = Σ_i E[X_i · 1_{A_i}] / 2^{i+1}, where A_i = {X_0 > … > X_i}.\n\n3. **Compute each term.** Conditioning on X_i = x, the other X_0,…,X_{i-1} must lie in the ordered region x < X_{i-1} < … < X_0 < 1, which has volume (1−x)^i / i!. Hence E[X_i · 1_{A_i}] = ∫₀¹ x(1−x)^i / i! dx = 1/(i+2)!.\n\n4. **Sum the series.** E[S] = Σ_{i≥0} 1/(2^{i+1}(i+2)!) = 2 Σ_{n≥2} (1/2)^n / n! = 2(e^{1/2} − 1 − 1/2) = 2√e − 3, which matches `putnam_2022_a4_solution`.\n\n**Why I stopped:** A Lean proof needs (a) turning `iIndepFun` plus `IsUniform` into a product law on finite coordinate tuples, (b) the iterated ordered-region integral in step 3, (c) interchanging the `tsum` with the integral (dominated convergence, using boundedness in [0,1]), and (d) identifying the exponential series. Each of these is substantial in Mathlib, and I can't read Mathlib sources here to find the right lemma names, so I'd be debugging blind for a long time."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":808,"cache_read_input_tokens":1922,"cac